WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:04.380
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:10.730
This work is about creating virtual characters
with a skeletal system, adding more than 300

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:17.190
muscles and teaching them to use these muscles
to kick, jump, move around and perform other

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:19.120
realistic human movements.

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:23.210
Throughout the video, you will see the activated
muscles with red.

00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:29.940
I am loving the idea, which, turns out, comes
lots of really interesting corollaries.

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:35.260
For instance, this simulation realistically
portrays how increasing the amount of weight

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:40.810
to be lifted changes what muscles are being
trained during a workout.

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:46.390
These agents also learned to jump really high
and you can see a drastic difference between

00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:51.350
the movement required for a mediocre jump
and an amazing one.

00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:56.060
As we are teaching these virtual agents within
a simulation, we can perform all kinds of

00:00:56.060 --> 00:01:01.460
crazy experiments by giving them horrendous
special conditions, such as bone deformities,

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:09.049
a stiff ankle, muscle deficiencies and watch
them learn to walk despite these setbacks.

00:01:09.049 --> 00:01:14.249
For instance, here you see that the muscles
in the left thigh are contracted, resulting

00:01:14.249 --> 00:01:19.810
in a stiff knee, and as a result, the agent
learned an asymmetric gait.

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:25.889
If the thigh-bones are twisted inwards, ouch,
the AI shows that it is still possible to

00:01:25.889 --> 00:01:28.880
control the muscles to walk in a stable manner.

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:34.799
I don’t know about you, but at this point
I am feeling quite sorry for these poor simulated

00:01:34.799 --> 00:01:39.709
beings, so let’s move on, we have plenty
of less gruesome, but equally interesting

00:01:39.709 --> 00:01:41.130
things to test here.

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:45.140
In fact, if we are in a simulation, why not
take it further?

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:46.929
It doesn’t cost anything!

00:01:46.929 --> 00:01:52.180
That’s exactly what the authors did, and
it turns out that we can even simulate the

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:53.359
use of prosthetics.

00:01:53.359 --> 00:01:58.959
However, since we don’t need to manufacture
these prosthetics, we can try a large number

00:01:58.959 --> 00:02:04.149
of different designs and evaluate their usefulness
without paying a dime.

00:02:04.149 --> 00:02:06.069
How cool is that?

00:02:06.069 --> 00:02:12.640
So far, we have hamstrung this poor character
many-many times, so why not try to heal it?

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:17.819
With this technique, we can also quickly test
the effect of different kinds of surgeries

00:02:17.819 --> 00:02:20.150
on the movement of the patient.

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:25.819
With this, you can see here how a hamstring
surgery can extend the range of motion of

00:02:25.819 --> 00:02:34.740
this skeleton.

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:39.280
It also tells us not to try our luck with
one-legged squats.

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:40.769
You heard it here first folks.

00:02:40.769 --> 00:02:44.629
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

